What is a Lambda Expression?
----------------------------
A lambda expression is a concise way of representing an anonymous function (a function without a name).
A lambda expression is not a method. It is an implementation of the functional interface's single abstract method.
(parameters) -> { method body }

Lambda expressions work with Functional Interfaces — interfaces that have exactly one abstract method.
    Examples:
    - Runnable
    - Comparator
    - Predicate
    - Function
    - Consumer
    - Supplier

What is a Functional Interface?
    A Functional Interface is an interface that contains exactly one abstract method.

    @FunctionalInterface?
    It is an annotation that tells Java that this interface is intended to have exactly one abstract method

    @FunctionalInterface
    interface Test {
        int calculate(int a);
        int add(int a, int b);  // compile-time error: multiple abstract methods
    }

    public class Main {
        public static void main(String[] args) {

            Test t = a -> {
                System.out.println(a);
                return a * 2;
            };

            System.out.println(t.calculate(5)); // method call - Executes implementation.
        }
    }

 Functional Interface defines WHAT needs to be done/defines contract. vs Lambda Expression defines HOW to do it.

 Without curly braces:
 ---------------------
 Test t = a -> a * 2;  Java automatically returns the expression's result.

 With curly braces:
  -----------------
 Test t = a -> {
     return a * 2;  // must return but no need of return when the functional interface method is void
 };

 Lambda                                  body	    Return required?
 a -> a * 2	                             Yes       implicit return
 a -> { return a * 2; }	                 Yes       explicit return
 a -> { System.out.println(a); }	     No        void operation
